Chapter: PrimTailCall
  cites Foreword chapter Console

 Tier 13: what a self tail call is allowed to do to its own arguments.

 Finding 33 gave the zig plug self-tail-call elimination -- a definition
 whose tail positions call itself at full arity becomes a loop, with the
 parameters as mutable locals. Bare metal has done this since Update 30
 and the python plug since the same commit. Three implementations of one
 transformation, and the transformation is only correct if it preserves
 a handful of properties no type checker states.

 This tier pins those properties. It is deliberately NOT about depth:
 whether a frame was reused is invisible from inside a program, and
 `stack_probe.py` is where that is measured. Every row here would be
 answered identically by an arm that loops and an arm that recurses --
 which is exactly the point, because the rows an optimisation can break
 are the ones where it changes an ANSWER.

 The counts are small on purpose. A row that needed a hundred thousand
 turns to show its answer would be measuring the stack again.

 `arg-swap` is the row this tier exists for. When a loop's next arguments
 read the parameters the loop is about to overwrite, an implementation
 that assigns left to right builds the second argument out of the first
 one's NEW value. The classic answer is to evaluate every argument into a
 temporary before assigning any of them, and the classic bug is to skip
 that step, because it produces a plausible number rather than a crash --
 with `a` and `b` swapping, a wrong arm counts twice as fast and still
 terminates. The zig plug's own commit for this took the temporaries; the
 row is here so a future one cannot quietly stop.

 `acc-grows` is the same hazard in the shape the compiler actually writes
 it: an accumulator built FROM ITSELF. Lists mutate in place here, so `&`
 is what makes a copy, and reading the old accumulator after the new one
 has been assigned would silently drop or duplicate elements.

 There is NO under-applied row, and the hole is deliberate. A tail
 position holding a partial application -- the shape finding 36 is about
 -- needs a definition whose return type is itself a function, and the
 first draft's `make-adder : Integer -> (Integer, Integer -> Integer)`
 broke BOTH arms: bare metal dumped registers instead of answering, and
 the zig arm would not compile the closure return type. Whether that is
 a defect or a syntax error of mine is finding 36's question to settle
 with a minimal reproducer, not this file's to carry as a red row.

 `mutual` is the pair no arm flattens: two definitions that tail-call each
 other. Every TCO in the fleet is self-only -- bare metal's `is-self-call`
 included -- so this row is not expected to move, and its job is to keep
 answering correctly if some future arm starts flattening it, and to say
 so loudly if flattening breaks it. Finding 37 is the standing reason to
 care.

 `unused-param` covers the discard path: zig rejects a local nothing
 reads, so a loop variable for a parameter the body never mentions has to
 be discarded rather than dropped, and dropping it would change which
 argument each remaining parameter receives.

 Expected on bare metal:

   arg-swap 15
   acc-grows 1 2 3 4
   mutual even
   unused-param 12
   control-nontail 55

Section: Shapes

 Two parameters that trade places every turn. `b` reads `a`, and `a` is
 assigned first, so an arm without temporaries reads the new `a` here.

  swap-loop : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  swap-loop (a) (b) (n) =
   if n == 0 then a + b
   else swap-loop b (a + 1) (n - 1)

 The accumulator is built from itself. `&` copies; `list-push` would
 mutate the list the caller still holds.

  acc-loop : List Integer, Integer -> List Integer
  acc-loop (acc) (n) =
   if n > 4 then acc
   else acc-loop (acc & [n]) (n + 1)

 Mutual tail recursion, which nothing here flattens today.

  is-even : Integer -> Boolean
  is-even (n) = if n == 0 then True else is-odd (n - 1)

  is-odd : Integer -> Boolean
  is-odd (n) = if n == 0 then False else is-even (n - 1)

 `tag` is never read by the body; it still has to survive as a parameter
 so that `n` and `acc` keep receiving the arguments meant for them.

  unused-loop : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  unused-loop (tag) (n) (acc) =
   if n == 0 then acc
   else unused-loop tag (n - 1) (acc + 3)

 The control: the call is inside an addition, so it is NOT in tail
 position and must keep its frames and its answer. An arm that loops this
 one has discarded the pending add.

  sum-nontail : Integer -> Integer
  sum-nontail (n) =
   if n == 0 then 0
   else n + sum-nontail (n - 1)

Section: Driver

  show-list : List Integer, Integer, Text -> Text
  show-list (xs) (i) (acc) =
   if i >= list-length xs then acc
   else show-list xs (i + 1) (acc & " " & show (list-at xs i))

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni ("arg-swap " & show (swap-loop 0 0 15))
    print-line-uni ("acc-grows" & show-list (acc-loop [] 1) 0 "")
    print-line-uni ("mutual " & (if (is-even 10) then "even" else "odd"))
    print-line-uni ("unused-param " & show (unused-loop 99 4 0))
    print-line-uni ("control-nontail " & show (sum-nontail 10))
  end
